response:
 To write a C++ program that implements the bubble sort algorithm using minimal memory and with error avoidance, follow these steps:

First, let's focus on implementing the bubble sort algorithm in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

void bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    size_t n = arr.size();
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i) {
        for (size_t j = 0; j < n - i - 1; ++j) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                std::swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::string jsonStr = R"({"array":[5,2,9,1,5,6]})"; // JSON string

    // Parse JSON string to get the array
    nlohmann::json j = nlohmann::json::parse(jsonStr);
    std::vector<int> arr = j["array"].get<std::vector<int>>();

    bubbleSort(arr);

    // Print sorted array
    for (auto elem : arr) {
        std::cout << elem << " ";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This code uses `nlohmann/json` library for parsing the JSON string, so make sure to install it before running the code. This implementation minimizes memory usage by using a vector instead of an additional array for swapping elements during sorting. It also avoids potential errors by using range-based for loops and checking if the swapping is needed before actually swapping the elements.

