 I'd be happy to help you understand a logical syllogism with an example! A syllogism is a kind of logical argument consisting of two propositions, called premises, and a conclusion that is logically deduced from those premises. Here's an example of a classic syllogism:

\begin{align*}
&\text{All men are mortal.} \\
&\text{Socrates is a man.} \\
&\therefore \text{Socrates is mortal.}
\end{align*}

In this example, the first proposition (premise) states that "all men are mortal." The second premise asserts that "Socrates is a man." The conclusion, which follows logically from the premises, is "Socrates is mortal."

The logical structure of this syllogism follows the form of the Aristotelian syllogism:

\begin{align*}
&\text{All } A \text{ are } B \\
&\text{C is } A \\
&\therefore C \text{ is } B
\end{align*}

In our example, we have \(A = \text{men}\), \(B=\text{mortal}\), and \(C=\text{Socrates}\). This syllogism demonstrates the valid inference from general to specific, where the conclusion logically follows from the given premises.

