 To make the given code more efficient, here are some suggestions:

1. Memoize the sorted `prompts` array: Since the sorting operation is an expensive computation and doesn't change frequently, memoizing the sorted array can help improve performance. You can create a custom hook or use React.memo for this.

```javascript
const sortedPrompts = useMemo(() => {
  if (!prompts.data) return [];
  return prompts.data.sort((a, b) => b.updatedAt.getTime() - a.updatedAt.getTime());
}, [prompts.data]);
```

2. Use the `useCallback` hook to memoize functions that don't change often: This can prevent unnecessary re-renders of child components.

```javascript
const handleEditPrompt = useCallback((p) => {
  setSelectedTargetPrompt(p.id);
  setEditedPrompt(p.prompt);
  setEditedName(p.name);
  onOpen();
}, [onOpen]);
```

3. Use the `useEffect` hook to fetch data only when the component mounts or certain state variables change: In your current implementation, the `api.prompt.all` query is fetched every time the component renders, which might not be necessary in all cases.

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  if (!prompts.isFetching && !prompts.isError) {
    // Fetch data or perform other actions
  }
}, [prompts.isFetching, prompts.isError]);
```

