Three Pivotal Moments During World War I:
1. The Battle of the Marne (September 6-12, 1914, near Paris, France): This battle marked the end of the German advance towards Paris and the beginning of trench warfare. The German Schlieffen Plan, which aimed to quickly defeat France before turning to Russia, relied on a swift advance through Belgium and France. However, the French and British forces were able to rally and stop the German advance at the Marne River. One lesser-known factor that contributed to the outcome was the poor communication and coordination within the German high command, which led to delays and confusion in the execution of the plan.
2. The Battle of Verdun (February 21, 1916 – December 18, 1916, near Verdun, France): This prolonged and bloody battle is considered one of the longest and deadliest battles in World War I. The Germans launched an offensive against the fortified town of Verdun with the goal of wearing down the French army and forcing them to surrender. While the Germans initially made gains, they ultimately failed to achieve their objective due to heavy casualties and the tenacity of the French defenders. A lesser-known factor that contributed to the outcome was the role of artillery, which played a significant role in the battle. Both sides used massive amounts of artillery to shell each other's positions, causing devastating damage and taking a toll on morale.
3. The United States Enters the War (April 6, 1917): The US entered the war on the side of the Allies after Germany resumed unrestricted submarine warfare, sinking several American merchant ships. This move by Germany was intended to disrupt British supply lines and force them to make peace. However, it had the opposite effect, leading the US to declare war on Germany. The entry of the US into the war shifted the balance of power in favor of the Allies and allowed them to eventually emerge victorious.

